   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the education law, in relation to the educational requirements for occupational therapists and occupational therapy assistants",
    "basePrintNo": "S7226",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7226-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 63,
       "fullName": "Timothy M. Kennedy",
       "imgName": "429_timothy_m._kennedy.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 429,
       "sessionMemberId": 712,
       "sessionYear": 2015,
       "shortName": "KENNEDY"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§7904 & 7904-a, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S7226\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the education law, in relation to the educational\nrequirements for occupational therapists and occupational therapy\nassistants\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nTo allow occupational therapists and occupational therapy assistants\nto hold greater educational credentials for licensing then currently\nin statute.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1. Amends section 7904 of the education law to allow for an\napplicant for a license as an occupational therapist to have a\nbaccalaureate or masters degree or greater.\n\nSection 2. Amends section 7904-a of the education law to allow for an\napplicant for a license as an occupational therapy assistant to have\nan associate degree or greater.\n\nSection 3. Sets for the effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nThe nationally recognized accrediting agency for the profession of\noccupational therapy, the American Occupational Therapy Association's\nAccreditation Council for Occupational Therapy Education, has\ndetermined that educational programs for occupational therapists may\ngrant either a masters degree or a doctorate degree.  In addition,\neducational programs for occupational therapy assistants may grant\neither an associate degree or a baccalaureate degree. This proposed\namendment to the requirements for education for an occupational\ntherapist and an occupational therapy assistant will allow the\ndepartment flexibility in granting a license to an applicant who has\ncompleted an education that exceeds the current requirements for a\nlicense.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone to the State.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect on the one hundred twentieth day after it\nshall have become a law; provided, however, that if chapter 470 of the\nlaws of 2015 shall not have taken effect on or before such effective\ndate, then section two of this act shall take effect on the same date\nand in the same as chapter 470 of the law of 2015 takes effect.\n",
